      "math_academy_id": "q-72842",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the second solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=-1,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={90}^{∘}-{180}^{∘}=-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
