    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-338740",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles",
      "problem": "The solution to the equation $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3=0$ for ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘}$ is $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)={0}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${0}^{∘}$ does not lie inside the required domain, we must find other solutions.\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find other solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find a solution in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1\\cdot {360}^{∘}={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={360}^{∘}$ is the only solution.",
